WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.234
Defenders use the Cyber Kill Chain for more than incident labels.

00:00:04.234 --> 00:00:07.991
It can organize timelines, map detection coverage, compare preventive controls,

00:00:07.991 --> 00:00:10.622
structure tabletop exercises, support threat intelligence reporting,

00:00:10.622 --> 00:00:14.004
and explain an incident to leaders in plain language.

00:00:14.004 --> 00:00:15.883
Its usefulness depends on flexibility.

00:00:15.883 --> 00:00:19.265
Modern activity may be nonlinear, cloud-native, identity-driven, automated, insider-enabled,

00:00:19.265 --> 00:00:21.144
or hidden inside legitimate tools.

00:00:21.144 --> 00:00:23.023
Some stages may be compressed.

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:24.150
Some may repeat.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:27.532
Some may not be visible with the telemetry available.

00:00:27.532 --> 00:00:31.290
Common mistakes include treating the model as a rigid checklist,

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:35.799
assuming every incident must map cleanly, equating one alert with full understanding,

00:00:35.799 --> 00:00:39.557
or focusing only on malware while ignoring identity, cloud, SaaS,

00:00:39.557 --> 00:00:40.684
and business-process evidence.

00:00:40.684 --> 00:00:43.690
The model works best when it improves questions.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:45.194
What have we observed?

00:00:45.194 --> 00:00:46.697
What are we inferring?

00:00:46.697 --> 00:00:48.576
What confidence do we have?

00:00:48.576 --> 00:00:49.703
Which controls helped?

00:00:49.703 --> 00:00:50.830
Which gaps mattered?

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:51.958
What should change?

00:00:51.958 --> 00:00:55.715
Use it alongside incident response practice, threat modeling, telemetry engineering,

00:00:55.715 --> 00:00:58.346
control validation, intelligence analysis, and business context.

00:00:58.346 --> 00:01:03.607
A good Kill Chain discussion should leave the team with clearer evidence, better priorities,

00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:05.110
and defensible next steps.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.492
A healthy use of the model also invites disagreement.

00:01:08.492 --> 00:01:13.753
If one analyst maps behavior to exploitation and another sees it as delivery evidence,

00:01:13.753 --> 00:01:17.886
the team should compare facts rather than force consensus too early.

00:01:17.886 --> 00:01:22.020
That discussion often reveals missing telemetry or assumptions that deserve documentation.
